---
title: Migrate the Nx Cloud Public API to v1
description: Update beta Nx Cloud Public API integrations to v1 paths and follow canonical response links.
filter: 'type:Guides'
topics:
  - 'Nx Cloud'
  - 'Continuous integration'
---

Move integrations from `/nx-cloud/data/v0-beta/` to `/nx-cloud/data/v1/` after your deployment publishes v1.
Check your deployment's `/nx-cloud/data/openapi.json` before you change a client.
Deployments can release v1 at different times.

## Check the deployed version

Use the host that serves your workspace.
The following example requires `curl` and `jq`:

```shell
export NX_CLOUD_HOST="https://cloud.nx.app"

curl --fail --silent --show-error \
  "$NX_CLOUD_HOST/nx-cloud/data/openapi.json" \
  --output openapi.json

jq -r '.info.version, (.paths | keys[])' openapi.json
```

If the specification still lists beta paths, keep your existing client paths until v1 becomes available.
Once v1 is available, use the [Public API reference](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/public-api) to check its
parameters, response fields, and errors.

## Update request paths and follow links

Replace the version segment in hard-coded Public API paths:

```plaintext
/nx-cloud/data/v0-beta/cipes
    → /nx-cloud/data/v1/cipes

/nx-cloud/data/v0-beta/runs/{runId}/tasks
    → /nx-cloud/data/v1/runs/{runId}/tasks
```

The beta and v1 routes use the same handlers during the transition.
Beta routes do not redirect requests to v1.
Response `links` and asset URLs use canonical v1 paths once your deployment publishes v1, including
responses to beta requests.

Follow the returned `links` and `assets[].url` values instead of reconstructing child paths.
Resolve root-relative URLs against your Nx Cloud host.
Update URL validators or allowlists that accept only `/v0-beta/` paths.

Keep your existing credential set.
Use a CI access token, or send both a personal access token and Nx Cloud Id as described in the
[authentication guide](/docs/kb/authenticate-public-api).
For signed asset downloads, keep credentials on the Nx Cloud request and omit them from the storage request.

## Check deprecation notices

A deployment can add these headers to beta responses after it publishes the migration guide and
approves the launch time:

| Header                          | Meaning                                                                                              |
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Deprecation`                   | The approved launch time, expressed as `@` followed by Unix epoch seconds.                           |
| `Sunset`                        | The announced sunset time, 14 days after launch, expressed as a Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) HTTP date. |
| `Link` with `rel="deprecation"` | The published migration guide URL.                                                                   |

The headers do not appear on v1 responses or the OpenAPI document.
The absence of beta deprecation headers does not prove that v1 is unavailable.
Check the deployed specification.
The `Sunset` header does not itself remove beta routes.
Do not assume a route-removal date beyond the deployment's release notice.

## Verify the integration

Before switching production traffic:

1. Confirm that the deployed specification lists your required v1 paths.
1. Read a completed resource through both versions with the same credential and filters.
1. Compare the response bodies, including the returned links.
1. Follow a child link and an asset URL from the v1 response.
1. Confirm that pagination preserves the original endpoint and filters.

For pagination and secure asset downloads, see [Query the Nx Cloud Public API](/docs/kb/query-public-api).
